1
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:13,365
Let's jump into TCP
connection establishment.

2
00:00:13,365 --> 00:00:16,910
We talked about how TCP
is connection-oriented.

3
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:18,810
Here, we'll talk about what that

4
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:22,130
means and how it happens.

5
00:00:22,170 --> 00:00:26,930
Just to talk about
why we need this,

6
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:31,230
let's refresh what we discussed
with reliable transfer.

7
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:34,670
The goal there was to
allow an application to be

8
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:38,415
assured that what they
send is what is received.

9
00:00:38,415 --> 00:00:41,115
Same data, same order.

10
00:00:41,115 --> 00:00:44,730
We talked about how
TCP handles this.

11
00:00:44,730 --> 00:00:47,550
It uses the sequence number and

12
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:50,450
acknowledgment numbers
in the packets

13
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:52,570
themselves to be able to

14
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:56,090
indicate which bytes
are being covered by

15
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:01,665
this datagram and
which bytes to expect.

16
00:01:01,665 --> 00:01:05,470
Acknowledging which ones
the receiver has received.

17
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:08,570
We talked about re-transmission,

18
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:10,830
where if a sender

19
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:13,290
had already sent out a
whole bunch of bytes,

20
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:15,700
but get some indication whether

21
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:21,965
through a timeout or
through a duplicate ACK,

22
00:01:21,965 --> 00:01:25,070
then it can
re-transmit that data.

23
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:27,570
Then finally, we talked
about flow control.

24
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:29,405
This is information that

25
00:01:29,405 --> 00:01:33,410
each side is sharing
with the other to say,

26
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:35,390
here's the amount of data that

27
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:39,250
I'm willing to actually receive.

28
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:43,090
All of that is to say

29
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:46,250
that each side needs
to store state.

30
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:50,070
Let's walk through an
example of a transmission.

31
00:01:50,070 --> 00:01:52,330
We have a process,
some application

32
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:56,210
that needs to transmit
400 bytes of data.

33
00:01:56,210 --> 00:02:01,910
It shares that with the
TCP, the transport layer,

34
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:03,970
which then is sending them,

35
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:08,135
but it will put that
inside of a send buffer.

36
00:02:08,135 --> 00:02:12,030
Those are transmitted
over the network.

37
00:02:12,030 --> 00:02:15,430
The receiver buffers that
up as it's receiving,

38
00:02:15,430 --> 00:02:17,270
such that it can deliver it

39
00:02:17,270 --> 00:02:19,720
to the process or application,

40
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:24,530
and any of the in-order
bytes that it has received,

41
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:26,790
it can deliver immediately

42
00:02:26,790 --> 00:02:29,165
or whenever the
process is ready,

43
00:02:29,165 --> 00:02:35,475
and it will acknowledge
the receipt of those data.

44
00:02:35,475 --> 00:02:40,490
But when the sender receives

45
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:45,470
this acknowledgment for 0-199,

46
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:49,055
basically acknowledgment
number was 200,

47
00:02:49,055 --> 00:02:51,890
the sender had to
have kept all of

48
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:56,230
that data in just in case
need to re-transmit,

49
00:02:56,230 --> 00:02:58,860
and in this case it does
need to re-transmit

50
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:06,310
the 299 datagram that
it previously sent.

51
00:03:06,310 --> 00:03:07,990
This is all state.

52
00:03:07,990 --> 00:03:12,450
The data in a buffer,

53
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:15,660
the receive windows for

54
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:17,260
the other side to know how

55
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:20,125
much the sender is
allowed to send,

56
00:03:20,125 --> 00:03:23,700
all of this information,
acknowledgment numbers,

57
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:29,225
the estimated RTT
or round trip time,

58
00:03:29,225 --> 00:03:31,135
all of that is state.

59
00:03:31,135 --> 00:03:37,160
Now that's where happens,

60
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:39,400
is you need to form
the connection such

61
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:41,820
that both sides agree, hey,

62
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:43,600
we're going to agree
to talk to each

63
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:47,320
other and we're fine now

64
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:50,600
establishing some
state because there's

65
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:55,140
a limited amount of resources
you have to store state.

66
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:58,600
This is where connection
establishment comes in.

67
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:02,285
We already said that TCP
is connection-oriented.

68
00:04:02,285 --> 00:04:04,320
The connection establishment is

69
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:05,900
an exchange of messages where

70
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:09,900
each process agrees
to form a connection.

71
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:13,700
This connection is
uniquely identified by

72
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:16,160
the source and
destination IP addresses

73
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:19,120
and the source and
destination ports.

74
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:22,775
It occurs through a
three-way handshake.

75
00:04:22,775 --> 00:04:27,535
Basically, this one side
to the other A-B says,

76
00:04:27,535 --> 00:04:30,435
I'd like to connect
the other side.

77
00:04:30,435 --> 00:04:36,920
B sends back to A.
I'm fine with that.

78
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,060
Then A sends to B

79
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:42,620
an acknowledgment of that
and basically says, thanks.

80
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:44,220
Now we'll get a little more

81
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:46,820
specific and concrete with this,

82
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:50,980
but that's conceptually
what it is.

83
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:55,180
Looking back at the TCP header,

84
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:58,465
we could see two
important fields here,

85
00:04:58,465 --> 00:05:01,660
or flags, they're
sometimes called.

86
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:05,470
It's a single bit
within the header.

87
00:05:05,470 --> 00:05:09,690
One is the ACK field and
one is the SYN field,

88
00:05:09,690 --> 00:05:15,915
S-Y-N. SYN indicates the
synchronized sequence number

89
00:05:15,915 --> 00:05:23,730
and say that the acknowledgment
number field is valid.

90
00:05:23,730 --> 00:05:25,350
We brushed over that when we

91
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:26,870
talked about the
acknowledgment number,

92
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:28,890
but that would need
to be set whenever

93
00:05:28,890 --> 00:05:31,650
the acknowledgment number
is actually being used.

94
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:34,690
But it's specifically called

95
00:05:34,690 --> 00:05:36,590
out here because it's
an important part

96
00:05:36,590 --> 00:05:39,025
of this connection
establishment.

97
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:44,610
Let's go through this
three-way handshake of TCP.

98
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:51,060
The host that's initiating
the connection,

99
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:54,195
in this case, it's
going to be host A.

100
00:05:54,195 --> 00:05:58,140
It will pick a starting
sequence number.

101
00:05:58,140 --> 00:06:02,910
We'll arbitrarily pick the
sequence number to be 1,000,

102
00:06:02,910 --> 00:06:07,470
and it will set the SYN flag

103
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:13,095
to indicate that this is the
start of that connection.

104
00:06:13,095 --> 00:06:15,580
It'll set the SYN flag and set

105
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:19,105
the sequence number to what
it chose, which is 1,000.

106
00:06:19,105 --> 00:06:21,240
B will receive that,

107
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:23,020
create some state on its side,

108
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:26,120
but then send back a message.

109
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:30,705
It will acknowledge the message

110
00:06:30,705 --> 00:06:32,920
or the datagram that
it just received.

111
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:35,840
It will say, raise
that ACK flag and

112
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:38,760
set the ACK number to be 1,001.

113
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:41,680
That's the next sequence
number I'm expecting.

114
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:44,310
This is what would be same,

115
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:49,260
and then B would also say
pick its own sequence number.

116
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:52,535
In this case it's picking 600.

117
00:06:52,535 --> 00:06:56,685
Then set the SYN flag as well,

118
00:06:56,685 --> 00:07:00,480
and set the sequence
number to be 600.

119
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:04,275
This is telling A,

120
00:07:04,275 --> 00:07:10,005
any traffic that B
is sending to A,

121
00:07:10,005 --> 00:07:13,655
it will start at
sequence number 600.

122
00:07:13,655 --> 00:07:17,290
Then finally host A acknowledges

123
00:07:17,290 --> 00:07:21,790
that message from B and sets

124
00:07:21,790 --> 00:07:23,610
the acknowledgment flag and

125
00:07:23,610 --> 00:07:25,150
the acknowledgment number to be

126
00:07:25,150 --> 00:07:30,075
the next byte in the sequence
or 601 that it's expecting.

127
00:07:30,075 --> 00:07:35,190
That's the three-way handshake
that you'll see in TCP.

128
00:07:35,190 --> 00:07:38,370
Once this happens, both
sides are now ready

129
00:07:38,370 --> 00:07:42,270
to transfer data
between each other.

130
00:07:42,550 --> 00:07:45,810
Now, what happens if packets are

131
00:07:45,810 --> 00:07:49,190
lost during the
connection establishment?

132
00:07:49,310 --> 00:07:52,290
What's the mechanism there?

133
00:07:52,290 --> 00:07:56,530
Let's look through what
would happen in each case.

134
00:07:56,530 --> 00:07:57,730
There's only three cases,

135
00:07:57,730 --> 00:07:59,770
so we can actually
enumerate them.

136
00:07:59,770 --> 00:08:03,140
Let's say if Packet 1 was lost,

137
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:07,935
so A won't ever get
an acknowledgment,

138
00:08:07,935 --> 00:08:12,030
and so it'll time out
and it will resend.

139
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:13,750
Now the estimated RTT has

140
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:16,590
no historical data
and so it'll just use

141
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:25,070
some default enough time

142
00:08:25,070 --> 00:08:27,990
that it can assume
that this would have

143
00:08:27,990 --> 00:08:29,610
been enough time to make

144
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:32,190
it a round trip
time to anywhere.

145
00:08:32,450 --> 00:08:35,730
That's if Packet 1 was lost,

146
00:08:35,730 --> 00:08:37,770
A would just reinitiate

147
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:41,030
the connection.
All is good there.

148
00:08:41,030 --> 00:08:43,610
Now let's say the
first packet went

149
00:08:43,610 --> 00:08:46,770
through and then this
second packet was lost.

150
00:08:46,770 --> 00:08:49,935
Now A again wouldn't
receive that

151
00:08:49,935 --> 00:08:54,105
ACK for which
acknowledged Packet 1.

152
00:08:54,105 --> 00:08:57,850
The same thing would happen
as if Packet 1 was lost.

153
00:08:57,850 --> 00:09:03,380
It will reinitiate
that connection,

154
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:05,525
resending Packet 1,

155
00:09:05,525 --> 00:09:08,130
and then B would just

156
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:11,925
acknowledge that
retransmitted Packet 1.

157
00:09:11,925 --> 00:09:17,775
We're good there. Let's
say the third one is lost,

158
00:09:17,775 --> 00:09:21,715
B won't get acknowledgment
for Packet 2,

159
00:09:21,715 --> 00:09:24,605
and so it will rescind Packet 2,

160
00:09:24,605 --> 00:09:29,655
and then A will acknowledge
the retransmitted Packet 2.

161
00:09:29,655 --> 00:09:32,790
In all cases, it's pretty
straightforward to

162
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:36,215
handle this and with this,

163
00:09:36,215 --> 00:09:38,110
the connection is
established and

164
00:09:38,110 --> 00:09:40,630
they're ready to communicate.

165
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:46,270
That ends the connection
establishment.

166
00:09:46,270 --> 00:09:51,430
We'll keep talking about
TCP next time. Thanks.